# gfx-demo

A picture made of nothing but mm.gfx's primitives. The scene is composed one
bit deep: a dithered glow that thins from the centre to the corners, a tunnel
of nested frames, a haloed porthole with a starburst of rays inside it, and an
off-centre tick so the turn is visible. Ordered dithering, the circle, and the
disc are pixel() and one-row rectangles in loops; the rays are lines from a
compile-time sine table. The frame turns a quarter clockwise four times.

A one-bit panel receives exactly that, black on white, one refresh per
orientation. A sixteen-bit panel receives the same bits expanded through six
rainbow bands on deep blue paper that turn with the scene and cycle their hues
sixteen times per orientation, and a plasma drawn directly in RGB565, masked to
a disc, written through the porthole and moving with the bands. The packed
composition is identical on both depths; only the write differs.

The application uses mm.gfx, mm.display, and mm.mcu. It has no font, board,
SDK, or vendor dependency.
